{align:justify|In a city in northern Thailand there was a noodle shop famed for its extraordinarily delicious broth. Its fragrance spread far and wide, and everyone flocked to eat there. The gourmets of the day all sang its praises. So although the shop worked only in the mornings, it sold several hundred bowls every morning and was sold out clean before noon. Each bowl cost about 15 to 20 Thai baht. Daily takings came to some 6,000, with 3,000 profit.}
{align:justify|The shop had started as a small roadside stall, but as profits grew, within just a few short years the owners bought land and built a spacious new restaurant. Behind it lay a large flower garden — to rest there on a quiet afternoon after the morning trade was truly paradise on earth.}
{align:justify|The owners, surnamed Lien, were a couple over sixty with both sons and daughters, all of them married. The whole family of five joined forces in the business and hired no outside help. Their broth tasted far superior to any rival's; connoisseurs were enraptured at the first sip. So from high officials and merchants down to market vendors and cyclo drivers, all became long-standing regulars.}
{align:justify|Rival shops could not master the recipe and tried in vain to pry out the trade secret. As the saying goes, "fellows of a trade are foes" — and it is no lie. Failing to extract the secret of the broth, the rival shops spread a spiteful rumor: "That shop puts opium in its broth — eat it once and you're hooked; you have to have it every day, you can't do without it."}
{align:justify|Someone went further still, quietly and secretly reporting to the Health Department, firmly insisting that the broth contained opium.}
{align:justify|Opium is a banned poison, so the Health Department at once sent inspectors to test the shop. In the end their conclusion was: "There is no opium or harmful chemical of any kind. The broth fully matches the owner's own account — it is made from vegetable juices combined with chicken stock and pork bones, simmered over a low flame for a full day and night to reach the required standard."}
{align:justify|Cleared and certified by the Health Department as having "NOTHING" in it, the shop grew more famous than ever and more beloved than ever — without spending a penny, it had been advertised for free, and the fame of its broth flew ever farther...}
{align:justify|There was a prince of the Thai royal family, a renowned culinary expert, who drove to the shop to taste it. Afterward he awarded it a medal and a special citation to hang before the door for all to admire. With this "golden plaque" from the prince, the owners' prestige rose higher still: each bowl of noodles went from 15 baht up to 20 or 25, and the Lien family's business prospered more than ever.}
{align:justify|Nothing in this world stays secret forever, and in the end the Lien family's jealously guarded broth monopoly was exposed.}
{align:justify|The one who lifted the veil on the secret was old Lien himself, and this strange and unprecedented news shook all of northeastern Thailand.}
{align:justify|The first to discover the secret was a cyclo driver named Tung. That day at lunch, Tung had drunk so much wine that his face was flushed and he could not take passengers; having no money to hand over to his wife, he dared not go home. That night, with his stomach growling, he slipped in through the shop's back door, thinking to scrounge something to line his belly.}
{align:justify|When he had pried open the back door and stepped into the kitchen, dim light glowed from afar — just enough for him to see someone sitting with his back to the door, which is to say, facing him. Looking closer, he realized it was the shop owner at work. Tung boldly crept nearer for a peek and saw clearly: old Lien was killing frogs. The old man's technique for slitting bellies and removing innards was masterful. He sorted everything into plastic tubs and plastic bags; the tubs were filled with frogs' corpses.}
{align:justify|Just then the owner seemed to sense someone and suddenly turned to look toward the door. Tung hid himself in time, and thanks to the dim light he was not spotted.}
{align:justify|Though the owner did not see Tung, Tung, hidden in the darkness, saw old Lien's face with perfect clarity in the lamplight. At once he nearly fainted with fright: the owner's face was not a human face at all, but a huge frog's face. The mouth was exactly like a frog's, the eyes bulged, and the sounds he made were a bizarre "krek-krek." Suddenly Tung saw him stick out a long tongue and snap it back in — exactly like a frog catching prey. Then he quickly withdrew inside.}
{align:justify|Tung was so terrified he wanted to scream and run for his life, but his legs only trembled and would not obey him. With great difficulty he pulled himself together and crawled out the door.}
{align:justify|That night he was so frightened he came down with a raging fever. Days later, recovered, he told everyone everything he had witnessed that night.}
{align:justify|But since he was known as a drinker, no one would believe a word he said. In truth, to people in northeastern Thailand, eating frogs, rats, cats and the like is commonplace — nothing strange about it.}
{align:justify|What was strange about the rumor was the honorable owner's face. For months now, diners had no longer seen old Lien show himself. Where he used to sit at the front of the shop chopping chicken, now word was he was sick in hospital, and no one knew when he would be discharged. If anyone did catch sight of him, it was only a fleeting glimpse; lately he seldom stepped into the shop.}
{align:justify|Those who happened to meet him reported:}
{align:justify|"The old owner's face looks exactly like a frog's — only much bigger. His legs are bent like a frog's too. The mouth is identical, the eyes bulge — the more you look, the more he resembles a giant frog."}
{align:justify|At this, everyone believed Tung had told the truth. Without a word they conspired to spy until they caught sight of the "man turned frog" that their owner had become. And in the end everyone learned that the irresistible broth was frog soup — strangely, improbably sweet and clear.}
{align:justify|Before, fearing the secret would leak, the owner had dared hire no workers and would let no other hand do the work, so he had to kill every frog himself. Over a lifetime old Lien had killed more lives than anyone could count — truly innumerable. Every day he faced the faces of frogs; his every act and gesture was entangled with frogs. As the mind is, so the form becomes; the outward shape follows the heart — and gradually his own face changed into a frog's shape.}
{align:justify|At first those living with him did not notice, until by the time it was discovered he had been utterly transformed. Later, the owner's relatives told how: "Lately, whenever old Lien falls asleep, he screams and roars, wailing that frogs are swarming all over his body, biting and tearing at him. The moment he dozes off they plague him so, and he can never rest in peace."}
{align:justify|From then on, for months on end, every night in bed he was visited by hosts of frogs that tormented him with pain. In the end old Lien went mad, croaking like a frog, and died.}
{align:justify|Afterward, the whole family of that once-famous broth shop vanished, none knows where — leaving behind only this story, a topic people still discuss to this day.}
